        cap("F6", "C11 emission from PilIR for every opcode and value physics (pilot emit c)", ::Draft,
            "selfhost/transpile/source.pilot + c_family.pilot: scalars are C scalars with the seed's wrapping and checked division; every other value is the runtime's tagged pilot_val, converted at each boundary (ISourceDialect.represent), with scalar destinations that a string, record or engine result reaches widened first (widen_mixed_kinds); blocks are labels and branches gotos; function values call through per-function wrappers and a dispatch table; pilot test --emit c: 170 passed / 0 failed / 0 at a seam, 3 not eligible (SEED001); struct values are C structs of their field types but heap-allocated and reached through pilot_val; a string's length is 64 bits (kept in the value up to PILOT_V_SHORT_MAX, else before the bytes; cli_f6 strings_past_the_short_length_limit_keep_their_length runs string programs with the limit at 4 bytes)", "emit.c_dynamic_values"),
        cap("F6", "struct values laid out as C structs of their fields' physics", ::Draft,
            "PilIR's record table (IrModule.records, printed `record <layout> <field layouts>`, read back by pilot ir roundtrip) gives each struct layout the layouts of its fields (selfhost/ir/lower_members.pilot note_record); the C emitter declares pilot_r<layout> with each field in its C type (an i64 an int64_t, an f64 a double), builds it with pilot_mk<layout> and reads and writes fields through typed accessors (also on a value carried as a dynamic one, such as a list element, by the layout the IR gave it before widening: SourceUnit.declared; cli_f6 a_struct_read_from_a_list_uses_its_typed_fields), falling back to the runtime's per-layout descriptors (pilot_rt_layouts) for a value of another layout and for printing and equality; tests/conformance/types/records_native.pilot; cli_f6 struct_values_are_c_structs_of_their_field_types; a struct value is still one heap allocation reached by reference, carved from 64 KiB chunks and reused through per-size free lists once freed (pilot_alloc_obj, pilot_free_obj), so benchmarks/native/records.pilot runs at about 10x C", "emit.c_dynamic_values"),
        cap("F6", "C runtime library with a stable ABI (allocation, strings, lists, maps, records, function values, printing, panics, arguments, standard input)", ::Draft,
            "runtime/c/pilot_rt.h, included by every emitted C file: pilot_val and the pilot_v_* / pilot_<engine op> entry points behave as the seed (printing, equality, ordering, run-time error messages), checked by the whole corpus through pilot test --emit c and crates/pilot-cli/tests/cli_f6.rs; concatenation appends in place when the left string ends at its buffer's written end (tests/conformance/memory/string_append_sharing.pilot), struct values come from 64 KiB chunks with per-size free lists; strings, lists, maps and struct values are reference counted and freed (see the F7 row); a string slice is a copy (a string's bytes start its buffer, whose header holds the count)", "emit.c_dynamic_values"),
        cap("F6", "C runtime strings: 16-byte values with a 4-byte prefix, short strings held in the value, word and SIMD equality, ordering, hashing and rare-byte-pair search", ::Draft,
            "runtime/c/pilot_rt_str.h (included by pilot_rt.h): a string value is a tag, a 24-bit header ((length << 1) | non-ASCII bit), its first 4 bytes and either its bytes 4..11 (strings of up to 12 bytes need no buffer) or its buffer's address with bit 63 marking a counted buffer (literals are static and never counted); equality compares header and prefix in one step, then the inline payload or overlapping 4/8-byte words, SSE2/NEON 16-byte steps and an AVX2 128-byte loop (chosen at run time with __builtin_cpu_supports); ordering compares byte-swapped prefix and inline words and finds the first differing byte with SSE2/NEON movemask and ctz; map keys hash 16 bytes a step (128-bit multiply mix); find anchors on the needle's two rarest bytes by the byte rank table (stdlib/core/byte_rank.pilot, measured by tools/byte_rank.pilot, written into each module by selfhost/transpile/c_family.pilot byte_rank_c), scans 32 positions a step with AVX2, else SSE2/NEON 16 or a borrow-free 64-bit SWAR 8, and switches to Knuth-Morris-Pratt when verifying outruns scanning, so it stays linear; a non-ASCII needle is never searched for in ASCII text; contains, starts_with, ends_with, trim (Unicode White_Space) and replace are C engine operations now (and Python and JavaScript helpers); tests/conformance/text/str_kernels and str_text_ops on the seed, pilot test --ir, --emit c (with --live-allocations), --emit python and --emit js; crates/pilot-cli/tests/cli_f6.rs runs str_kernels with PILOT_V_SHORT_MAX=4 and builds benchmarks/native/string_compare, string_find and string_map against their C references; the core str descriptor (stdlib/core/primitives/str.pilot) still declares a { ptr<u8>, usize } view: this layout is the C backend's only", "emit.c_dynamic_values"),
